\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Matching one plaquette coefficient}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Consider a single #{U(1)} plaquette variable with the
[normalized Wilson or Villain weight](fdip-007D). Write
#{r(\beta)=I_1(\beta)/I_0(\beta)}. The coefficient series makes
#{r>0}; the integral of #{(1-\cos\theta)e^{\beta\cos\theta}}
is strictly positive, so #{r<1}. Matching the fundamental coefficient
therefore determines a unique positive parameter
##{\beta_V(\beta)=-\frac1{2\log r(\beta)}.}
At this parameter the Villain coefficient at #{n} is
#{r(\beta)^{n^2}}, whereas the Wilson coefficient is
#{I_n(\beta)/I_0(\beta)}.}

\p{At #{\beta=2}, the convergent series gives
##{r\approx0.697774657964008,\qquad
 \beta_V\approx1.38943282120604.}
The first coefficients are
##{\begin{array}{c|c|c}
n&\widehat c_n^W&\widehat c_n^V\\\hline
0&1&1\\
1&0.697774657964008&0.697774657964008\\
2&0.302225342035992&0.237061359207224
\end{array}}
The negative coefficients equal the corresponding positive ones.
These numbers are evaluations of the exact series in
\ref{fdip-007D}; for example, at #{\beta=2} the ratio of successive
terms of #{I_n} is #{1/((j+1)(j+|n|+1))}, providing a geometric
bound on the remaining tail once #{j} is fixed.}

\p{For either normalized even weight,
#{\mathbb E[\cos(n\theta)]=\widehat c_n}. Thus the two models
agree on the fundamental cosine expectation and disagree on the
second harmonic. They are different probability laws and different
actions, even after adjusting the coupling and discarding additive
action constants. This is a one-plaquette comparison, not an assertion
about a many-plaquette continuum limit.}
